WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.860
IPC messages are local API calls with a serialization boundary.

2
00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:10.808
A secure contract should define message types, required fields, optional fields, version identifiers, allowed values, encoding assumptions, size

3
00:00:10.808 --> 00:00:12.352
limits, and unsupported behavior.

4
00:00:12.352 --> 00:00:18.914
Without that contract, receivers tend to grow ambiguous defaults and hidden assumptions that become difficult to review.

5
00:00:18.914 --> 00:00:26.634
Every message should be treated as untrusted until validated, even when it comes from another component on the same machine.

6
00:00:26.634 --> 00:00:33.968
The receiver should check the message type, version, required fields, numeric ranges, string lengths, identifiers, and state requirements before

7
00:00:33.968 --> 00:00:35.126
using the data.

8
00:00:35.126 --> 00:00:40.916
Unknown commands and unsupported versions should fail predictably, with safe diagnostics and no partial action.

9
00:00:40.916 --> 00:00:43.232
Validation should happen before sensitive operations.

10
00:00:43.232 --> 00:00:51.724
Do not allocate large buffers, open files, use elevated operations, mutate state, or pass data to downstream components until the message has

11
00:00:51.724 --> 00:00:53.268
satisfied the boundary contract.

12
00:00:53.268 --> 00:00:59.444
If a message contains nested structures, validate the outer frame and limits before parsing deeper content.

13
00:00:59.444 --> 00:01:02.146
Accepted messages should become narrow internal types.

14
00:01:02.146 --> 00:01:09.094
That conversion separates raw IPC input from trusted application requests and helps reviewers see exactly what was accepted.

15
00:01:09.094 --> 00:01:15.656
It also reduces the chance that a partially validated field or ambiguous default slips into business logic.

16
00:01:15.656 --> 00:01:23.376
Good message contracts make local communication ordinary to maintain because the allowed behavior is visible in code, tests, and documentation.

17
00:01:23.376 --> 00:01:25.692
Compatibility should be deliberate as well.

18
00:01:25.692 --> 00:01:34.956
If a new client sends a newer message, the receiver should know whether to reject it, ignore an optional field, or require an upgrade.

19
00:01:34.956 --> 00:01:38.430
Silent interpretation of unknown behavior makes IPC review fragile.

20
00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:46.922
Contract tests should include old clients, new clients, missing fields, extra fields, and unsupported commands in the wrong state during upgrades and

21
00:01:46.922 --> 00:01:48.080
rollbacks too safely.
